 Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 27 Jan 2026 (this version), latest version 22 Mar 2026 (cite5†v2 )]\nL9: # Title:LinguaMap: Which Layers of LLMs Speak Your Language and How to Tune Them?\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†J. Ben Tamo , cite7†Daniel Carlander-Reuterfelt , cite8†Jonathan Rubin , cite9†Dezhi Hong , cite10†Mingxian Wang , cite11†Oleg Poliannikov L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled LinguaMap: Which Layers of LLMs Speak Your Language and How to Tune Them?, by J. Ben Tamo and 4 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite12†View PDF L16: > Abstract:Despite multilingual pretraining, large language models often struggle with non-English tasks, particularly in language control, the ability to respond in the intended language. We identify and characterize two key failure modes: the multilingual transfer bottleneck (correct language, incorrect task response) and the language consistency bottleneck (correct task response, wrong language).\nL17: To systematically surface these issues, we design a four-scenario evaluation protocol spanning MMLU, MGSM, and XQuAD benchmarks. To probe these issues with interpretability, we extend logit lens analysis to track language probabilities layer by layer and compute cross-lingual semantic similarity of hidden states.\nL18: The results reveal a three-phase internal structure: early layers align inputs into a shared semantic space, middle layers perform task reasoning, and late layers drive language-specific generation. Guided by these insights, we introduce selective fine-tuning of only the final layers responsible for language control.\nL19: On Qwen-3-32B and Bloom-7.1B, this method achieves over 98 percent language consistency across six languages while fine-tuning only 3-5 percent of parameters, without sacrificing task accuracy. Importantly, this result is nearly identical to that of full-scope fine-tuning (for example, above 98 percent language consistency for both methods across all prompt scenarios) but uses a fraction of the computational resources.\nL20: To the best of our knowledge, this is the first approach to leverage layer-localization of language control for efficient multilingual adaptation.\nL21: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL22: Cite as:  | cite13†arXiv:2601.20009 [cs.CL]\nL23:    | (or cite14†arXiv:2601.20009v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL24:    | cite15†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2601.20009†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Junior Ben Tamo [cite16†view email ]\nL28: [v1] Tue, 27 Jan 2026 19:38:12 UTC (733 KB)\nL29: cite5†[v2] Sun, 22 Mar 2026 02:37:29 UTC (538 KB)\nL30: \nL31: Full-text links:\nL32: \nL33: ## Access Paper:\nL34: \nL35: View a PDF of the paper titled LinguaMap: Which Layers of LLMs Speak Your Language and How to Tune Them?, by J. Ben Tamo and 4 other authors\nL36: \nL37:   * cite12†View PDF L38:   * cite17†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite18†view license†creativecommons.org L41: ### Current browse context:\nL42: \nL43: cs.CL\nL44: \nL45: cite19†< prev |   cite20†next > L46: \nL47: cite21†new | cite22†recent | cite23†2026-01 L48: \nL49: Change to browse by:\nL50: \nL51: cite24†cs L52: cite25†cs.AI L53: cite26†cs.LG L54: \nL55: ### References & Citations\nL56: \nL57:   * cite27†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L58:   * cite28†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L59:   * cite29†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L60: \nL61: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL62: \nL63: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL64: \nL65: [Button: ×]\nL66: \nL67: loading...\nL68: \nL69: Data provided by:\nL70: \nL71: ### Bookmark\nL72: \nL73: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL74: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL75: \nL76: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL77: \nL78: Bibliographic Explorer (cite30†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL79: \nL80: [Inpu